From 2ae6b29d81ac3bfa0baca30e8b38d5134b32c950 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Lepikhov <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:22:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH v1 1/2] Make EXPLAIN ANALYZE report the number of unmatched
 outer rows

This provides information about the number of tuples on the outer side
of the join that have not found a matching partner on the inner side.
A large number of partnerless outer rows may point at a missed index or
at a suboptimal join tree planning.

ExecNestLoop(), ExecHashJoinImpl() and ExecMergeJoin() already track,
per outer tuple, whether it has found a partner satisfying the join keys
and the Join Filter (the *_MatchedOuter flag).  The flag used to be set
on every matching pair. It is now set on the first one only, and that
transition is counted in ntuples2, which join nodes did not use so far.
show_join_outer_counts() derives the unmatched count as the outer
child's row count minus the matched one.  InstrAggNode() already sums
ntuples2, so parallel workers are covered.

Like the actual row count, the value is averaged over loops and printed
with two decimal places.  The divisor is the join node's loop count, as
for "Rows Removed by Join Filter", because the line belongs to the join
node; a loop that never reads the outer child, as a hash join with an
empty hash table may do, simply contributes zero.  In text format the
line is shown only when it is not zero, like "Rows Removed by ...", so
the plans of most existing queries do not change; other formats always
include it.  Hash Right Semi Join shows nothing: it skips already
matched inner tuples before testing the join qual and so cannot tell
matched outer rows.
